            <ArticleTitle>[Giant mesenteric lipoma in children: A case-report].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>457-459</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0929-693X(17)30085-4</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.arcped.2017.02.022</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Mesenteric lipoma is an extremely rare disease in children. Fewer than 50 cases have been reported in the literature. Diagnosis is based on clinical examination, ultrasound, and computed tomography (CT). However, only the histological study of the specimen during laparotomy or laparoscopy can confirm the diagnosis. Thus, surgery, be it by laparotomy or laparoscopic, is both a means of exploration and treatment in mesenteric lipoma. We report on a case of giant lipoma of the mesentery in a 7-year-old girl presenting paroxysmal abdominal pain with a subocclusive syndrome lasting 1 week.</AbstractText>
